#04  Fourier's integral
===================================================================================================

  Joseph Fourier, in Théorie analytique de la chaleur (1822),
  basically demonstrated that any signal
  can be decomposed into a sum of sinusoids
  of various amplitudes, frequencies and phases.
  //  https://archive.org/details/thorieanalytiq00four/page/n7/mode/2up
  //  https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Fourier

  These sinusoids are called partials and their sum is called spectrum.
  /*
    EDIT —— woops:
      That's not what a spectrum is (forgive me, I thought so for years).
      Sinusoids are the time-domain representation of the signal.
      Spectrum is a frequency-domain concept:
      it's the amplitude and phase per frequency of a signal.
      I'm not sure that what I've erroneously called a spectrum thus far
      has any other name than just "signal"...
      Except: representing it as a sum of sinusoids
      is in fact making use of its spectral data.
      Pardon the long interruption
      but I struggle at seeing frequency and time domains
      as separate hermetic entities ——
      —— theoretically, Bartók's Concerto for orchestra
      is one single waveform —— I guess my ears suck at maths then.
      https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spectrum#English

      Frequency without time is a division by zero.
      If you sort your underwear by colour,
      the colours don't start having a separate autonomous existence.
      The equal signs in the [SPOILER] Laplace and Fourier transforms [/SPOILER]
      are not figures of speech.
  */
  If all partials have their frequencies related by an integer factor,
  they are called harmonics.
  Then the one with the lowest frequency
  is called fundamental
  and all the other ones are called overtones.
  In such a case, the composite signal is periodic
  and its frequency is the fundamental's frequency
  thus called fundamental frequency.

  In case of a periodic signal,
  the sum is discrete:

     ω₂  =  2·ω         //
     ω₃  =  3·ω         //  where ω
    ...                 //  is the fundamental frequency
    ω_n  =  n·ω         //

  and can therefore be effectively expressed as a literal sum:

             ∞
    ƒ(t)  =  ∑ α[n]cos( nωt + φ[n] )    [7]
            n=0

  [Partials-split VCO]  https://www.desmos.com/calculator/seevdzs1xi

  Notice that ω is a constant.
  α[n]  means that each partial has its own amplitude
  without necessarily being mathematically related to n.
  (It's an index, not necessarily a function —— although it could be.
  It could also be = 0.)
  Same goes for  φ[n] .
  The partials don't have to be in phase for the signal to be periodic.
  /*
    I thought so. I checked, I was wrong.
    If the partials are not in phase,
    the period will have a random shape
    but it will be repeated at the fundamental frequency.
    Which is pretty obvious once you start thinking about it...
    but first you have to start thinking about it.
  */

  [Pseudo-noise]  https://www.desmos.com/calculator/w0dxrgh6kj

  /*
    //  Digression: what happens when  n = 0 ?
    ////  DC offset
    In an analogue circuit,
    the fact that  α cos(ωt + φ)  changes sign each half-period
    corresponds to the current changing direction,
    as is normal with AC (alternating current).
    //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alternating_current
    A direct current (DC) can be either positive or negative
    but its sign (polarity) doesn't change.
    //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Direct_current

    When  n = 0 :

