  //  Sinusoids
  Sinusoids have a modulus (period) of 2π ——
  —— their curve is a unique motif repeated identically ad infinitum.

  Sine starts at zero;
  it rises up to 1 between zero and π/2;
  then it falls down to zero between π/2 and π.
  This is the first half-period,
  it has a vertical axis of symmetry at π/2.
  So the area under the curve, during this half-period,
  is split in two equal halves by this axis.
  So the areas under the curve of the two first quarter-periods
  are equal to each other.
  Then the second half-period is identical to the first one,
  except mirrored across the abscissa (horizontal) axis.
  So the two last quarter-periods are also symmetrical,
  and also have equal area under the curve;
  and the full period has a centre of symmetry at (0 , π) ——
  —— and the two half-periods have complementary areas under the curve.
  So the area under the curve of each full period cancels to zero.
  Which means that integrating sine over a full period outputs zero.

  Cosine starts at one,
  falls down to to zero (π/2),
  then to minus one (π),
  then rises up to zero (3π/2),
  then back to one (2π).
  So it has an axis of symmetry at the middle of its full period
  and a centre of symmetry at the middle of each half-period.
  So the areas under the curve of the two first quarter-periods
  cancel to zero ——
  —— idem for the two last quarter-periods.
  So the area under the curve of a full period adds zero to zero.
  Which means that integrating cosine over a full period outputs zero.

  Complex sinusoids in the form:  exp(jx)
  have a magnitude (absolute value) of 1
  and an argument of x.
  So, in the complex plane,
  they run along the unit-circle at a constant angular speed.
  When  x = π  the argument is half a circle.
  The half-circle is above the real axis.
  Each quarter is on one side of the imaginary axis,
  so the real area cancels out,
  and the imaginary area is strictly positive.
  When  x = 2π  the argument is a full circle.
  The second half-circle is below the real axis
  and also split in the middle by the imaginary axis.
  So the last two quarter-circles also cancel the real area.
  The two half-circles have the same absolute imaginary area,
  but the first one is positive and the second one is negative;
  so integrating the full circle outputs zero.
  Complex sinusoids reset at 2π ——
  —— they have the same modulus (period) as real sinusoids.

  Sine has a specificity:
  between zero and π, it's positive;
  between zero and —π, it's negative.
  So its full infinite curve
  has a centre of symmetry at the origin of the Cartesian plane.
  Which means that its right semi-plane and its left semi-plane are complementary.
  So integrating sine from  —k  to  +k  (where k is any real positive number)
  will always output zero.
  Therefore, integrating a complex sinusoid from  —k  to  +k
  (i.e. in both directions)
  will always output a real number.

  If k is an integer factor of π  ( k = nπ , n ∊ ℤ ),
  the span:  k —  —k  =  2k  =  n2π
  is an integer factor of 2π,
  then:  2k  mod 2π  =  0 .

  Therefore:
    *integrating phasors over a bilateral window
    spanning an integer factor of π
    on both sides of the origin
    outputs *zero**.

